Patch 7928 (fix, online): sys_net_abort_socket ends a receive blocked on that socket (EINTR), and errno is per thread, as on a PS3.

After a lost opponent (the other PC quit mid-fight, "Connection has timed out." box, OK), the next Online Battle sat on
"Communicating..." for good. The main thread was not waiting on the server: J-Stars' P2P teardown (0x46A2B8, reached from the
server-select step 0x47253C -> 0x459124) sets its receive thread's stop flag, calls sys_net_abort_socket(fd, 0), then
sys_ppu_thread_join on that thread ("UDPP2P Recive Thread", blocked in recvfrom on the P2P UDP socket; it leaves when errno is EINTR),
then shutdown and socketclose. sys_net_abort_socket was not implemented (no export, the call did nothing), the host recvfrom never
woke, and the join never returned ("[WAIT] ppu_thread_join(tid=25)", the main thread's trace stops).
libs/network/sysNet.c:
- sys_net_abort_socket is exported; a blocking recv/recvfrom waits in poll slices of at most 100 ms (wait_readable) and returns -1 / EINTR
  when the socket is aborted. The poll still returns the moment a datagram arrives, so a receive is no slower.
- The abort is not lost: it stays pending (abort_gen / abort_seen per socket) until a blocking receive consumes it, so it also ends a
  receive that starts just after it (the moment between the title's stop-flag check and its recvfrom), and the abort state and the
  SO_RCVTIMEO deadline are taken once per call, not once per pass of recvfrom's loop over psnr's own packets. Every receive waiting on
  the socket at that moment returns EINTR; alloc_slot clears a pending abort, so a reused fd never inherits one.
- socketclose and sys_net_finalize_network also end a receive blocked on the socket with EINTR (what the blocking host recv gave on
  Windows when another thread closed the socket), and each slice checks that the slot is still in use with the same host socket, so a
  waiter never reads the data of a socket that took over its fd.
- SO_RCVTIMEO is kept per socket and ends the wait with EWOULDBLOCK after the time asked for, measured on a monotonic clock (each slice
  waits min(100 ms, what is left)). On Windows a receive timeout now gives EWOULDBLOCK (35), as on a PS3 and as Linux already did,
  where Winsock's own timeout gave ETIMEDOUT (60). The guest value is converted saturating (guest_timeout_us, also used for
  SO_SNDTIMEO): sec * 1000000 + usec no longer wraps, and anything past 2^32 - 1 ms (about 49.7 days) means no timeout, as such a
  timeout never runs out on a PS3. The socket keeps the value only when the host setsockopt took it.
- A poll interrupted by a signal (EINTR) loops back to the abort check instead of falling into a blocking host recv; any other poll
  error is returned. Nonblocking sockets and MSG_DONTWAIT are unchanged.
- errno (the _sys_net_errno_loc cell and the value behind it) is per thread. The receive thread tests errno == EINTR at the top of
  its loop, before its first recvfrom: with one errno for every thread, the EINTR that ended one session's receive thread ended the
  next session's at once, and the host never heard its new opponent (the joiner left the room every ~15 s). Guest scratch (the errno
  cells among it) is allocated under a lock, since the runtime's bump allocator is not thread-safe and two threads' first errno calls
  could otherwise get the same cell.
- an accepted socket's slot keeps the listener's blocking mode (what Winsock's accept gives the host socket), so wait_readable
  sees the mode the socket really has.
libs/network/tests/test_sys_net.c: a recvfrom blocked on another thread ends with EINTR in that thread's errno when the socket is
aborted, the main thread's errno is untouched, and a new receive thread starts at errno 0 and still receives; an abort with no receiver
waiting ends the next receive, once; SO_RCVTIMEO gives EWOULDBLOCK in well under a 100 ms slice; closing the socket ends a blocked
receive; an abort lands while recvfrom loops past a psnr packet. The receivers have a 5 s SO_RCVTIMEO and start in step with the main
thread (no sleep to win a race), so a lost abort fails the test instead of hanging it. Huge SO_RCVTIMEO values (one that used to
wrap to about 0.45 s, all ones, and just past 2^32 - 1 ms) leave the receiver waiting until an abort 0.7 s later ends it with EINTR.
Applies after 7927.

